'te.m.passadoT':'How the past was measured',
'te.m.p1':'<b>Mined area.</b> MapBiomas Collection 11 Mining class (30), 1985 to 2025. Where MapBiomas does not use that class (Catalão and Ouvidor), the area comes from ANM titles, adding classes 30 and 25.',
'te.m.p2':'<b>Origin of the area.</b> For each pixel that becomes a mine, its use the year before; native vegetation lost up to 3 years earlier counts as indirect conversion.',
'te.m.p3':'<b>Emissions.</b> Biomass from SEEG 12 stocks for Goiás (Forest 64.7; Savanna 41.3; Grassland 18.5; pasture 7.6 tC/ha), secondary vegetation at 44% of the stock and mines at zero. 0–30 cm soil from MapBiomas Soil Collection 2.',
'te.m.p4':'<b>Surroundings.</b> Event study with 40 mines opened from 1991 to 2015 (≥ 1 ha) and 1,500 ANM titles without a mine as controls, matched on native vegetation 6 and 11 years before opening.',
'te.m.futuroT':'How the scenarios and the risk were built',
'te.m.f1':'<b>Operations</b> expand at the rate observed from 2015 to 2025, inside their own polygon.',
'te.m.f2':'<b>New projects</b> follow the historical area distribution 10 years after opening in their mineral group (ANM titles opened from 1986 to 2015), capped by the title, with a linear ramp from the expected year. 2025 vegetation and soil carbon come from the 8 nearest sample points.',
'te.m.f3':'<b>Risk.</b> Each project has a potential impact range if it operates for 10 years (mine area, native vegetation cleared and emissions, at p5/p50/p95, plus the 0–5 km surroundings). The impact level (quartiles of the direct-plus-surroundings index) is crossed with the probability given by maturity.',
'te.m.causalT':'Causality: what has been tested',
'te.m.c1':'<b>Permutation inference.</b> 300 placebos drawing fake mines among the controls, with the same opening years. It confirms the effect in year −1 (p &lt; 0.01) and at +10 years in the 1–5 km rings (p between 0.02 and 0.05).',
'te.m.c2':'<b>Dose-response with ANM data.</b> Area intensity per Mt produced, by mineral. Area does not respond to production year by year, which fits step-wise expansions.',
'te.m.c3':'<b>Matching before the dip.</b> The baseline is 6 years before opening; with the baseline at year −1 the effect flips sign through regression to the mean, which shows why the choice matters.',
'te.m.fortalecerT':'How to strengthen the causal link',
'te.m.v1':'<b>Mining permit date as the event.</b> The Cadastro Mineiro event history gives the grant date: the event becomes administrative and precedes the satellite.',
'te.m.v2':'<b>International prices as an exogenous shock.</b> Cross each mineral’s price (World Bank, USGS) with each municipality’s geological endowment in a shift-share design.',
'te.m.v3':'<b>Closer controls.</b> Use titles that applied for mining and were refused or lapsed: they went through the same geological and economic selection.',
'te.m.v4':'<b>Distance and direction gradient.</b> Thinner rings and the direction of access roads (OpenStreetMap) test the infrastructure mechanism.',
'te.m.v5':'<b>More treated mines.</b> Include the titles where MapBiomas uses class 25 (Catalão) and extend the cohorts to 2020 with a +5-year horizon.',
'te.m.v6':'<b>Validation with high-resolution imagery.</b> Checking a sample of openings (Planet NICFI, Sentinel-2) measures the classification lag and omission error.',
'te.m.limT':'Limitations',
'te.m.l1':'MapBiomas does not use the Mining class in Catalão and Ouvidor and gets the substance wrong in Niquelândia and Minaçu. Both were corrected with ANM data, but there may be other cases.',
'te.m.l2':'Underground mines (Crixás) and sand dredging hardly show up from satellite.',
'te.m.l3':'The surroundings effect comes from 40 small and medium mines; applying it to large projects is an extrapolation.',
'te.m.l4':'For new projects, mine size follows the Goiás history; projects of unprecedented scale, such as rare earths, may be underestimated.',
'te.m.l5':'Vegetation around the projects was interpolated from sample points (mean distance to the points up to 15 km for 83% of projects).',
'te.m.l6':'Soil carbon assumes all removed carbon is emitted; part of the stripped soil is kept for rehabilitation.',
'te.m.proxT':'Squad 2 next steps',
'te.m.n1':'Extract 2025 vegetation inside and around each Radar project in Earth Engine and replace the interpolation with real values.',
'te.m.n2':'Download the Cadastro Mineiro event history and redo the lag and the event study with the permit date.',
'te.m.n3':'Link to the Squad 2 demand model: projected production × ha per Mt × tCO₂ per ha, by mineral.',
'te.m.n4':'Shift-share design with prices for mining expansion and deforestation by municipality.',
'te.m.n5':'Validate the maturity rules (what goes into each scenario) with the team and review the 57 very-high-risk projects by hand.',
